HUB.add({
  id: 'webpack', title: 'Webpack and build tools', short: 'Webpack',
  blurb: 'Entry, output, loaders, plugins, splitting, HMR, tree shaking. Notes on Vite and Rspack.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is Webpack?`,
  k: `A packing machine: it takes all your files and packs them into a few neat boxes the browser can carry.`,
  a: `A module bundler: starting from entry points it builds a dependency graph of JS, CSS, images and fonts, transforms them with loaders, optimises with plugins, and outputs bundles. Still common in existing apps (and in Next.js before Turbopack).`,
  u: `New React apps mostly use Vite (esbuild + Rollup/Rolldown); Rspack is a fast Webpack-compatible alternative.` },
{ q: `What are the main features of Webpack?`,
  k: `Packing, changing file types, splitting into boxes, and refreshing instantly while you work.`,
  a: `Dependency graph bundling, loaders for any file type, plugin system, code splitting and dynamic imports, tree shaking, asset modules, dev server with HMR, source maps, environment modes, caching (persistent filesystem cache), Module Federation for micro-frontends.` },
{ q: `How do you set up a basic Webpack configuration?`,
  k: `Write a note saying where packing starts, where boxes go, and how to read each file.`,
  a: `Install <code>webpack webpack-cli</code>, add <code>webpack.config.js</code> with entry, output, module rules and plugins, then run <code>webpack --mode production</code>.`,
  c: `module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.jsx',
  output: { path: __dirname + '/dist', filename: '[name].[contenthash].js', clean: true },
  module: { rules: [{ test: /\\.[jt]sx?$/, exclude: /node_modules/, use: 'babel-loader' }] },
  resolve: { extensions: ['.js', '.jsx', '.ts', '.tsx'] },
  plugins: [new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ template: './public/index.html' })],
}` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of entry points in Webpack?`,
  k: `The first door where packing begins.`,
  a: `The module(s) where Webpack starts building the dependency graph. One entry for SPAs; multiple entries for multi-page apps or separate bundles (app, admin).` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of output in Webpack?`,
  k: `Where the packed boxes are put and what they're called.`,
  a: `Configures the output directory, file names (use <code>[contenthash]</code> for long-term caching), <code>publicPath</code> for asset URLs, chunk naming and library format.` },
{ q: `How do you handle different file types in Webpack?`,
  k: `Give the machine special glasses for each kind of file.`,
  a: `Add <code>module.rules</code> matching by <code>test</code>: babel/swc loaders for JS/TS, css/style loaders for CSS, <b>asset modules</b> (<code>type: 'asset/resource'</code> or <code>'asset'</code>) for images and fonts, and loaders for SVG as components.` },
{ q: `What are loaders in Webpack?`,
  k: `Translators that turn each file into something the machine understands.`,
  a: `Functions that transform a source file before it's added to the graph: <code>babel-loader</code>, <code>ts-loader</code>, <code>css-loader</code>, <code>sass-loader</code>, <code>postcss-loader</code>. Chained right to left (last listed runs first).` },
{ q: `What are plugins in Webpack?`,
  k: `Helpers who work on the whole packing job, not one file.`,
  a: `Classes hooking into the compilation lifecycle for tasks loaders can't do: generate HTML, extract CSS, define env constants, analyse bundles, copy files, compress. Examples: HtmlWebpackPlugin, MiniCssExtractPlugin, DefinePlugin, BundleAnalyzerPlugin.` },
{ q: `How do you use the HtmlWebpackPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `It writes the front page and adds the right box names to it.`,
  a: `It generates <code>index.html</code> (from a template) and injects script/link tags for the hashed bundles automatically.`,
  c: `plugins: [new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ template: './public/index.html', favicon: './public/favicon.ico' })]` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the CleanWebpackPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `It throws away old boxes before packing new ones.`,
  a: `It empties the output folder before each build so stale hashed files don't pile up. Since Webpack 5 use <code>output.clean: true</code> instead.`,
  u: `Mostly unnecessary in Webpack 5.` },
{ q: `How do you handle CSS in Webpack?`,
  k: `Teach the machine to read paint files and put the paint on the page.`,
  a: `<code>css-loader</code> resolves imports and urls; <code>style-loader</code> injects styles in development; <code>MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader</code> extracts CSS files in production. Add <code>postcss-loader</code> (autoprefixer, Tailwind) and <code>sass-loader</code> if needed. CSS Modules via <code>css-loader</code> options or <code>.module.css</code> naming.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the MiniCssExtractPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `It puts all the paint into its own paint box instead of hiding it inside the code box.`,
  a: `Extracts CSS into separate files per chunk so CSS loads in parallel and caches separately, avoiding a flash of unstyled content. Pair with <code>css-minimizer-webpack-plugin</code>.` },
{ q: `How do you handle images and fonts in Webpack?`,
  k: `Pictures and letters get packed too, small ones inside, big ones as their own files.`,
  a: `Webpack 5 asset modules: <code>asset/resource</code> emits a file and returns its URL, <code>asset/inline</code> returns a data URI, <code>asset</code> chooses by size (<code>parser.dataUrlCondition.maxSize</code>). Replaces file-loader and url-loader.`,
  c: `{ test: /\\.(png|jpe?g|svg|woff2?)$/i, type: 'asset' }` },
{ q: `What is code splitting in Webpack?`,
  k: `Cutting the big box into smaller boxes so you only carry what you need now.`,
  a: `Splitting the bundle into chunks loaded on demand or in parallel: via multiple entries, dynamic <code>import()</code>, and <code>SplitChunksPlugin</code> for shared/vendor code. Reduces initial JS and improves caching.` },
{ q: `How do you implement code splitting in Webpack?`,
  k: `Mark which parts can come later.`,
  a: `Use dynamic <code>import()</code> (with <code>React.lazy</code> for components), configure <code>optimization.splitChunks</code>, and name chunks with magic comments.`,
  c: `const Chart = lazy(() => import(/* webpackChunkName: "chart" */ './Chart'))
optimization: { splitChunks: { chunks: 'all' }, runtimeChunk: 'single' }` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the SplitChunksPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `It finds toys used in many boxes and puts them in one shared box.`,
  a: `Built into Webpack 4+, it extracts shared modules and vendor code into separate chunks based on rules (size, number of requests, cacheGroups). <code>chunks: 'all'</code> is a good default.` },
{ q: `How do you optimize the build process in Webpack?`,
  k: `Pack faster: remember last time, skip what didn't change, use quicker translators.`,
  a: `Persistent cache (<code>cache: { type: 'filesystem' }</code>), faster transpilers (swc-loader, esbuild-loader), narrow <code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code>, fewer plugins in dev, <code>thread-loader</code> for heavy loaders, cheaper dev source maps, and type checking in a separate process. Or switch to Rspack/Vite.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the DefinePlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `It writes fixed words into the code while packing, like "this is the real shop".`,
  a: `Replaces expressions with constants at compile time, e.g. <code>process.env.NODE_ENV</code> or feature flags, enabling dead-code elimination. Values must be JSON-stringified. Never inject secrets: they end up in the browser bundle.`,
  c: `new webpack.DefinePlugin({ __API_URL__: JSON.stringify(process.env.API_URL) })` },
{ q: `How do you handle environment variables in Webpack?`,
  k: `Different addresses for practice and real shops, written in at packing time.`,
  a: `Read <code>process.env</code> in the config (dotenv), pass with DefinePlugin or <code>EnvironmentPlugin</code>, use <code>--env</code> or <code>--mode</code>. Anything injected is public. Vite equivalent: <code>import.meta.env.VITE_*</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the HotModuleReplacementPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `Swap one brick while the tower stays standing, instead of rebuilding the whole tower.`,
  a: `HMR replaces changed modules in the running app without a full reload, preserving state. In Webpack 5 the dev server enables it with <code>hot: true</code> (the plugin is added automatically). React uses React Refresh (<code>@pmmmwh/react-refresh-webpack-plugin</code>).` },
{ q: `How do you set up hot module replacement in Webpack?`,
  k: `Turn on the brick-swapping switch.`,
  a: `Use webpack-dev-server with <code>devServer: { hot: true }</code>, add <code>ReactRefreshWebpackPlugin</code> and the <code>react-refresh/babel</code> plugin in development.` },
{ q: `How do you use the Webpack DevServer?`,
  k: `A practice shop on your own computer that refreshes as you work.`,
  a: `<code>webpack serve</code> serves bundles from memory with live reload/HMR. Common options: <code>port</code>, <code>historyApiFallback: true</code> (SPA routing), <code>proxy</code> to your API (avoids CORS), <code>open</code>, <code>static</code>.`,
  c: `devServer: { port: 3000, hot: true, historyApiFallback: true, proxy: [{ context: ['/api'], target: 'http://localhost:4000' }] }` },
{ q: `What are source maps, and how do you configure them in Webpack?`,
  k: `A treasure map from the packed code back to the code you wrote, so you can find bugs.`,
  a: `Files mapping bundled code to original sources for debugging. Set <code>devtool</code>: <code>eval-cheap-module-source-map</code> in dev (fast), <code>source-map</code> or <code>hidden-source-map</code> in production (upload to error tracking, don't expose publicly if code is sensitive).` },
{ q: `How do you handle tree shaking in Webpack?`,
  k: `Shake the tree and the toys nobody uses fall out of the box.`,
  a: `Removing unused exports. Requirements: ES modules (not CommonJS), <code>mode: 'production'</code>, <code>"sideEffects": false</code> (or a list) in package.json, and Babel not converting modules. Import specific functions and prefer ESM-friendly libraries (lodash-es).` },
{ q: `How do you optimize the performance of a Webpack build?`,
  k: `Make the boxes smaller and easier to keep.`,
  a: `Production mode, content hashes, splitChunks + runtimeChunk, lazy routes, tree shaking, analysing with bundle analyzer, replacing heavy libraries, compressing (gzip/brotli), image optimisation, and long-term caching. For build speed, see caching and faster loaders.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the BundleAnalyzerPlugin in Webpack?`,
  k: `An x-ray of your boxes that shows what's taking up space.`,
  a: `<code>webpack-bundle-analyzer</code> shows an interactive treemap of bundle contents and sizes so you can find large or duplicated dependencies. Vite equivalent: <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code>.` },
{ q: `How do you handle multiple entry points in Webpack?`,
  k: `Two front doors, two sets of boxes.`,
  a: `Use an object entry; use <code>[name]</code> in output names, one HtmlWebpackPlugin per page with <code>chunks</code>, and splitChunks to share common code.`,
  c: `entry: { app: './src/app.jsx', admin: './src/admin.jsx' },
plugins: [
  new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ filename: 'index.html', chunks: ['app'] }),
  new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ filename: 'admin.html', chunks: ['admin'] }),
]` },
{ q: `What is the difference between development and production mode in Webpack?`,
  k: `Practice mode is quick and chatty; real mode is small and tidy.`,
  a: `<b>development</b>: fast rebuilds, readable output, detailed errors, eval source maps, no minification. <b>production</b>: minification (Terser), tree shaking, scope hoisting, <code>NODE_ENV=production</code> (React drops dev checks), deterministic ids.` },
{ q: `How do you configure Webpack for a React application?`,
  k: `Teach the machine about JSX, paint and the front page.`,
  a: `babel-loader with <code>@babel/preset-react</code> (<code>runtime: 'automatic'</code>) and preset-env (or swc-loader), CSS loaders, asset modules, HtmlWebpackPlugin, dev server with <code>historyApiFallback</code> and React Refresh, resolve extensions, and production optimisations.`,
  u: `For new React projects use Vite: npm create vite@latest. Zero config for all of the above.` },
{ q: `How do you handle polyfills in Webpack?`,
  k: `Add helper parts so old browsers can play with new toys.`,
  a: `Use <code>@babel/preset-env</code> with <code>useBuiltIns: 'usage'</code> and core-js plus a browserslist target. Webpack 5 no longer auto-polyfills Node built-ins; add <code>resolve.fallback</code> only if a library truly needs them. Modern targets need few polyfills.` },
  ],
});
